  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c77f7a71ccb7b4ad1598b09bf4fe585c82a7f614ebb4abe3f495643a746fcb3c.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2},$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*",
  "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9e6e1147d95c65465c802e8bfad5c4ac285dc9716d098452c41bdd6caed5b9ca.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(2-{x}^{2})-(-x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
